And I remember like describing like,

00:45:13.296 --> 00:45:15.963
what his experience of ADHD was,

00:45:17.017 --> 00:45:19.998
and I was just like,
"What a fucking liar!"

00:45:19.998 --> 00:45:22.793
Like, "What a fucking liar! I do that!

00:45:22.793 --> 00:45:25.011
And he's getting some
free time on an exam!

00:45:25.011 --> 00:45:26.010
What the fuck?"

00:45:26.010 --> 00:45:28.595
And it just like, 'cause
like it just didn't,

00:45:28.595 --> 00:45:30.061
it just, for some reason,

00:45:30.061 --> 00:45:31.670
it just never fucking occurred to me,

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:34.507
that unfortunately I
had the same symptoms,

00:45:34.507 --> 00:45:36.710
because I was raised ...

00:45:36.710 --> 00:45:38.547
not even raised, but like,

00:45:38.547 --> 00:45:40.367
a lot of people in my culture

00:45:40.367 --> 00:45:43.532
and the environment I grew up with,

00:45:43.532 --> 00:45:44.760
it was just, we were,

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:45.907
it was put in our minds that, you know,

00:45:45.907 --> 00:45:48.141
mental health just wasn't really like,

00:45:48.141 --> 00:45:50.775
it was either like a
very very serious thing,

00:45:50.775 --> 00:45:53.118
or it just like, was, just didn't exist.

00:45:53.118 --> 00:45:54.673
And then you've just gotta
get on with your day, right?

00:45:54.673 --> 00:45:55.917
- Like there's no such thing
as a mild symptom, right?

00:45:55.917 --> 00:45:57.192
- Yeah yeah yeah!

00:45:57.192 --> 00:45:58.184
- It's like you either had
it or you don't have it.

00:45:58.184 --> 00:45:59.453
- So what made you think,

00:45:59.453 --> 00:46:00.560
"Oh, I should go and see if I have it."

00:46:00.560 --> 00:46:01.728
Like, what was the turning point?

00:46:01.728 --> 00:46:02.561
Like what ...

00:46:02.561 --> 00:46:04.338
- This is gonna be awful.

00:46:04.338 --> 00:46:06.330
Enough comments on Trash Taste.

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:07.552
- No way! Are you serious?

00:46:07.552 --> 00:46:08.763
- Mentioning it. I am actually serious.

00:46:08.763 --> 00:46:09.596
- Wait, why?

00:46:09.596 --> 00:46:10.553
'Cause you were like,
fidgeting with things?

00:46:10.553 --> 00:46:11.386
- Huh?

00:46:11.386 --> 00:46:12.219
- 'Cause you were fidgeting with things?

00:46:12.219 --> 00:46:13.627
- Honestly, seeing myself
on camera, I'm just like ...

00:46:13.627 --> 00:46:16.678
Damn, man, I do that shit? What?

00:46:16.678 --> 00:46:19.613
But yeah. Because like, obviously
I've never been on camera,

00:46:19.613 --> 00:46:22.853
and especially with me being
on camera documented so much

00:46:22.853 --> 00:46:24.231
on Trash Taste, I'm like,

00:46:24.231 --> 00:46:25.467
damn, I do a lot of weird shit!

00:46:25.467 --> 00:46:27.134
- So people psychoanalyze us all the time.

00:46:27.134 --> 00:46:28.344
- Like I understand-

00:46:28.344 --> 00:46:29.828
- We start to notice habits, right?

00:46:29.828 --> 00:46:30.824
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:46:30.824 --> 00:46:31.868
I started to notice habits,

00:46:31.868 --> 00:46:34.579
'cause you're looking at yourself
through the third person.

00:46:34.579 --> 00:46:36.214
And then also, you know,

00:46:36.214 --> 00:46:38.027
there's always gonna be
comments that are like,

00:46:38.027 --> 00:46:39.799
psychoanalyzing and over-analyzing stuff.

00:46:39.799 --> 00:46:41.178
- Oh yeah, people say
some really wild shit.

00:46:41.178 --> 00:46:42.011
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:46:42.011 --> 00:46:45.095
But then like, I saw some
comments that are just like,

00:46:45.095 --> 00:46:47.358
fuck, they kind of make sense,

00:46:47.358 --> 00:46:48.379
and then I Googled it.

00:46:48.379 --> 00:46:50.769
Google what they say, I'm like damn,

00:46:50.769 --> 00:46:53.316
that doesn't look like it's just someone

00:46:53.316 --> 00:46:54.390
talking out their ass.

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:55.404
And then like the final straw

00:46:55.404 --> 00:46:57.609
was meeting someone here who has ADHD

00:46:57.609 --> 00:46:59.812
and they described their
symptoms, I'm like ...

00:46:59.812 --> 00:47:00.645
Fuck, okay.

00:47:00.645 --> 00:47:01.574
I need to go get diagnosed.

00:47:01.574 --> 00:47:03.699
I need to go get checked out.

00:47:03.699 --> 00:47:07.569
And yeah, it was thanks
to Trash Taste, I guess,

00:47:07.569 --> 00:47:09.474
and thanks to random YouTube comments.

00:47:09.474 --> 00:47:10.307
- Well done guys!

00:47:10.307 --> 00:47:11.229
- Well done guys!

00:47:11.229 --> 00:47:14.742
You're like, it's like the
one time in the world where

00:47:14.742 --> 00:47:16.986
you guys were actually
correct with your diagnosis.

00:47:16.986 --> 00:47:19.189
And you weren't just a bunch of like,

00:47:19.189 --> 00:47:20.999
Reddit armchair psychologists.

00:47:20.999 --> 00:47:22.005
- I've seen some wild ones.

00:47:22.005 --> 00:47:23.668
I see some wild diagnoses.

00:47:23.668 --> 00:47:25.143
- Yeah, I do. I have as well.

00:47:25.143 --> 00:47:26.926
- In a few years of Trash Taste.

00:47:26.926 --> 00:47:30.621
- That's not an excuse to
psychoanalyze all of us, okay?

00:47:30.621 --> 00:47:33.824
A broken clock is right
two times, you know guys?

00:47:33.824 --> 00:47:36.142
(laughing)

00:47:36.142 --> 00:47:38.376
- I remember my, fucking,

00:47:38.376 --> 00:47:39.430
when I was growing up,

00:47:39.430 --> 00:47:42.005
everyone was like, convinced
I had like, dyslexia.

00:47:42.005 --> 00:47:42.838
- Yeah.

00:47:42.838 --> 00:47:44.113
- And like, I was like,

00:47:44.113 --> 00:47:45.368
"No, I don't think I do.

00:47:45.368 --> 00:47:47.571
'Cause I don't really have
a problem reading the words.

00:47:47.571 --> 00:47:49.622
I just really fucking hate doing it."

00:47:49.622 --> 00:47:50.455
- Right.

00:47:50.455 --> 00:47:52.719
- But, 'cause they would
describe the symptoms to me

00:47:52.719 --> 00:47:54.741
as like a, you know like,

00:47:54.741 --> 00:47:56.072
when I was like 10 or something.

00:47:56.072 --> 00:47:57.940
So to give some back story to this,

00:47:57.940 --> 00:47:59.951
I went to a Welsh school.

00:47:59.951 --> 00:48:01.350
I didn't like reading.

00:48:01.350 --> 00:48:02.374
I liked gaming.

00:48:02.374 --> 00:48:03.294
I was probably-

00:48:03.294 --> 00:48:04.213
- Gamer from birth.

00:48:04.213 --> 00:48:05.382
- My parents probably wished that

00:48:05.382 --> 00:48:06.503
nobody had ever introduced me to gaming.

00:48:06.503 --> 00:48:09.841
But I was introduced to
gaming very very young.

00:48:09.841 --> 00:48:12.048
We're talking like, four, five, six.

00:48:12.048 --> 00:48:12.881
- Oh wow.

00:48:12.881 --> 00:48:13.714
- I found out about video games.

00:48:13.714 --> 00:48:14.697
And I became like, obsessed.

00:48:14.697 --> 00:48:16.250
And I read the games.

00:48:16.250 --> 00:48:18.590
I mean, I've said this before,

00:48:18.590 --> 00:48:19.539
I beat all of,

00:48:19.539 --> 00:48:20.634
like one of the first games I ever played

00:48:20.634 --> 00:48:21.845
was Pokemon Gold in French,

00:48:21.845 --> 00:48:23.263
and I beat the whole game in French.

00:48:23.263 --> 00:48:24.634
I don't know how.

00:48:24.634 --> 00:48:26.012
- Even though you don't know any French.

00:48:26.012 --> 00:48:27.370
- Yeah, this must have been literally

00:48:27.370 --> 00:48:29.185
the birth of me bashing
my head against the walls

00:48:29.185 --> 00:48:32.218
to figure out shit in
video games, not reading.

00:48:32.218 --> 00:48:33.993
So I've been doing this
since like a young age.

00:48:33.993 --> 00:48:36.560
I never really, like, read stuff.

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:37.872
I just kind of did it.

00:48:37.872 --> 00:48:39.548
This is probably like, sounds very like,

00:48:39.548 --> 00:48:40.456
monkey-esque already,

00:48:40.456 --> 00:48:41.714
and not, you know,

00:48:41.714 --> 00:48:42.900
not very intelligent.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:44.003
- It's very like, a primeval like-

00:48:44.003 --> 00:48:45.170
- Yeah, it is.

00:48:46.095 --> 00:48:47.536
And I went to a Welsh school.

00:48:47.536 --> 00:48:51.901
So I didn't really read
English, like ever.

00:48:51.901 --> 00:48:53.990
We learned everything in Welsh.

00:48:53.990 --> 00:48:55.478
I spoke in Welsh in school.

00:48:55.478 --> 00:48:57.061
So I never read English, like ever.

00:48:57.061 --> 00:48:58.511
The only thing I'd ever read is like,

00:48:58.511 --> 00:48:59.732
we go to the store, I can read the,

00:48:59.732 --> 00:49:01.126
I would just read the fucking-

00:49:01.126 --> 00:49:02.005
- [Joey] Bread.

00:49:02.005 --> 00:49:02.887
- Yeah, bread, whatever like.

00:49:02.887 --> 00:49:04.630
But like, when I would be quizzed on like,

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:06.900
how to spell shit, I like, wouldn't know.

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:08.030
They'd be like, "Spell said,"

00:49:08.030 --> 00:49:11.209
and I'd be like, "Oh yeah,
just Z. Z-E-D, innit? Said."

00:49:11.209 --> 00:49:12.530
I'd be like, I don't know.

00:49:12.530 --> 00:49:13.363
- Your.

00:49:13.363 --> 00:49:16.140
- Yeah. So like, this is from
like age eight or whatever.

00:49:16.140 --> 00:49:17.264
Like, I was fucking awful.

00:49:17.264 --> 00:49:18.911
And so when we started
learning English more,

00:49:18.911 --> 00:49:20.163
I was like, yeah,

00:49:20.163 --> 00:49:21.998
I just don't read books, ever.

00:49:21.998 --> 00:49:24.053
And they're like, "What?
You don't read books?"

00:49:24.053 --> 00:49:25.848
And my parents were
like, "Yeah, we tried to!

00:49:25.848 --> 00:49:27.075
We tried to make him read, he wouldn't."

00:49:27.075 --> 00:49:29.276
- But he keeps playing
those goddamn video games!

00:49:29.276 --> 00:49:30.359
- To be fair, like,

00:49:30.359 --> 00:49:33.552
my parents tried their goddamn
hardest to get me to read.

00:49:33.552 --> 00:49:34.835
It was just not gonna happen.

00:49:34.835 --> 00:49:37.469
Like I was just, I had
absolutely zero interest in it.

00:49:37.469 --> 00:49:40.266
So I was so so bad at English.

00:49:40.266 --> 00:49:42.479
But like, they knew I was very competent

00:49:42.479 --> 00:49:43.638
at all other subjects.

00:49:43.638 --> 00:49:45.577
Like I was very like, you know,

00:49:45.577 --> 00:49:46.894
maths and science and all that,

00:49:46.894 --> 00:49:48.044
really really high marks,

00:49:48.044 --> 00:49:49.381
like I was, you know,

00:49:49.381 --> 00:49:52.638
I mean I got like A-star and
shit in maths and all that,

00:49:52.638 --> 00:49:53.953
it's like the highest grade you can get.

00:49:53.953 --> 00:49:55.061
I went and did engineering and all that.

00:49:55.061 --> 00:49:56.674
So there's no problem with like,

00:49:56.674 --> 00:49:58.877
I wasn't like, bad at learning.

00:49:58.877 --> 00:50:01.290
It was just, I just
hated this one subject.

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:03.263
But they were convinced, they were like,

00:50:03.263 --> 00:50:04.744
"Well if he's competent in other stuff,

00:50:04.744 --> 00:50:07.014
he just must have some
dyslexia or something."

00:50:07.014 --> 00:50:09.106
And they would tell me
what the symptoms were.

00:50:09.106 --> 00:50:11.459
And it was so annoying, because as a kid,

00:50:11.459 --> 00:50:13.674
this is where I started learning where

00:50:13.674 --> 00:50:15.399
adults like, fuck with you as a kid.

00:50:15.399 --> 00:50:18.852
They would like try and
spoon-feed me the answers to like,

00:50:18.852 --> 00:50:20.180
to dyslexia.

00:50:20.180 --> 00:50:21.031
- Right.

00:50:21.031 --> 00:50:22.749
- They would like, try
and tell me I had symptoms

00:50:22.749 --> 00:50:23.906
that I didn't have.

00:50:23.906 --> 00:50:25.240
- And to like, get you to agree to it?

00:50:25.240 --> 00:50:26.779
- Just because they wanted to have like,

00:50:26.779 --> 00:50:28.436
so they can tick it off as like,

00:50:28.436 --> 00:50:29.416
"Oh, we've figured it out.

00:50:29.416 --> 00:50:30.381
We'll sort him out."

00:50:30.381 --> 00:50:31.696
And then whenever I have any issues,

00:50:31.696 --> 00:50:32.768
they'd be like, "God, don't worry,

00:50:32.768 --> 00:50:33.765
we don't have to put in more work.

00:50:33.765 --> 00:50:34.665
He's just dyslexic."

00:50:34.665 --> 00:50:35.527
You know what I mean? Kinda?

00:50:35.527 --> 00:50:36.511
This is where you like ...

00:50:36.511 --> 00:50:37.817
I get it, 'cause the teachers,

00:50:37.817 --> 00:50:38.831
they want an answer.

00:50:38.831 --> 00:50:39.761
They want a reason.

00:50:39.761 --> 00:50:40.594
- They wanted a solution, right?

00:50:40.594 --> 00:50:42.202
- They wanna know why,
and I can understand,

00:50:42.202 --> 00:50:44.843
everyone wants a reason why, but like,

00:50:44.843 --> 00:50:47.001
unfortunately mental
health and shit like that

00:50:47.001 --> 00:50:48.847
and learning is like,

00:50:48.847 --> 00:50:51.529
the most complicated shit known to man.

00:50:51.529 --> 00:50:54.598
And there is never ever just
a one solution that fits all.

00:50:54.598 --> 00:50:55.905
- Yeah like, just because
somebody sucks at something,

00:50:55.905 --> 00:50:58.420
it doesn't mean there's
an inherent problem

00:50:58.420 --> 00:50:59.646
with them, right?

00:50:59.646 --> 00:51:00.852
- So I would like, talk
to my teachers and stuff,

00:51:00.852 --> 00:51:02.314
and I remember talking
to my English teacher

00:51:02.314 --> 00:51:03.886
and she like, made me like,

00:51:03.886 --> 00:51:05.077
stay after class and she'd talk to me,

00:51:05.077 --> 00:51:05.936
and she'd be like,

00:51:05.936 --> 00:51:08.142
"So, you can read these words, right?"

00:51:08.142 --> 00:51:09.471
And I was like, "Yeah, I can read them."

00:51:09.471 --> 00:51:11.714
She's like, "They don't
look like messed up or

00:51:11.714 --> 00:51:12.796
jumbled at all?"

00:51:12.796 --> 00:51:15.766
And I was like, "No, not really."

00:51:15.766 --> 00:51:17.289
And then she was like,

00:51:17.289 --> 00:51:19.885
"Sometimes the words are hard to read,"

00:51:19.885 --> 00:51:22.352
I'm like, "Yeah, I don't
know the word sometimes.

00:51:22.352 --> 00:51:23.802
'Cause I don't really know it.

00:51:23.802 --> 00:51:25.139
So it's kind of hard to read,

00:51:25.139 --> 00:51:26.187
'cause English is kinda funny

00:51:26.187 --> 00:51:27.306
and the pronunciation is weird."

00:51:27.306 --> 00:51:29.215
And then, so she was like,

00:51:29.215 --> 00:51:30.048
"Hmm, okay okay."

00:51:30.048 --> 00:51:32.536
So then another teacher
came in a few weeks later

00:51:32.536 --> 00:51:33.735
and started asking me more questions

00:51:33.735 --> 00:51:34.807
about dyslexia and stuff.

00:51:34.807 --> 00:51:38.466
And I was like, "The words
are not fucking jumbled, okay?

00:51:38.466 --> 00:51:40.884
I've seen the movies, they
don't fucking do that!"

00:51:40.884 --> 00:51:42.317
And I know that's not how
it works, but I'm like,

00:51:42.317 --> 00:51:44.064
"I do not have a problem!

00:51:44.064 --> 00:51:45.874
I can see all the letters in order!

00:51:45.874 --> 00:51:47.933
They do not move, I know what I'm reading!

00:51:47.933 --> 00:51:49.504
I am just shit at this subject,

00:51:49.504 --> 00:51:50.586
and I don't like it!"

00:51:50.586 --> 00:51:52.042
- They just like, start
shaking your body like,

00:51:52.042 --> 00:51:52.904
"What about now?"

00:51:52.904 --> 00:51:53.795
- Yeah, yeah.

00:51:53.795 --> 00:51:56.242
It was literally like,

00:51:56.242 --> 00:51:59.500
thank god I wasn't a kid
that would just like,

00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:00.549
and I felt bad, 'cause like,

00:52:00.549 --> 00:52:03.123
kids are so easily like,
pushed into saying yes or no.

00:52:03.123 --> 00:52:05.908
I'm so glad I was a stubborn
fuck from like a young age.

00:52:05.908 --> 00:52:08.748
Where I was like, "No!
The words are not jumbled!

00:52:08.748 --> 00:52:10.575
I don't have a problem reading the words,

00:52:10.575 --> 00:52:12.138
I just hate this shitty subject!

00:52:12.138 --> 00:52:13.374
I hate English!"

00:52:13.374 --> 00:52:15.309
And they were like, "No no no no no!

00:52:15.309 --> 00:52:18.843
You have to be! You have to
have a disability or something!"

00:52:18.843 --> 00:52:19.822
- You got this fucked!

00:52:19.822 --> 00:52:21.045
- You must be! You must be!

00:52:21.045 --> 00:52:22.044
- Yeah, you must be!

00:52:22.044 --> 00:52:24.286
- I'm very glad that, I
don't know where I got

00:52:24.286 --> 00:52:25.626
the stubbornness from, but I was glad!

00:52:25.626 --> 00:52:26.977
'Cause like, I remember like,

00:52:26.977 --> 00:52:27.992
it was really annoying.

00:52:27.992 --> 00:52:29.101
And they kept insisting I had it.

00:52:29.101 --> 00:52:30.218
I'm like, no! No!

00:52:30.218 --> 00:52:31.738
- [Joey] It's like, stop!

00:52:31.738 --> 00:52:33.189
- I do not have this!

00:52:33.189 --> 00:52:34.994
I have no problem reading Of Mice and Men!

00:52:34.994 --> 00:52:36.437
I just don't like it!

00:52:36.437 --> 00:52:37.963
I'd rather watch the movie!

00:52:37.963 --> 00:52:39.860
- They're like, tryna just
like brainwash you, right?

00:52:39.860 --> 00:52:41.305
- Okay. So this is what I'm realizing.

00:52:41.305 --> 00:52:43.442
And I understand now why like,

00:52:43.442 --> 00:52:46.390
you know, this whole discussion
about diagnosing kids

00:52:46.390 --> 00:52:49.323
is like, I get why it's
like a touchy subject.

00:52:49.323 --> 00:52:52.701
Some parents are like, "My
kids! You can't diagnose them!"

00:52:52.701 --> 00:52:54.652
And then, you know, some people
are so quick to jump to it.

00:52:54.652 --> 00:52:57.570
It's a really uncomfortable
subject and kind of touchy,

00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:00.265
'cause the kids often
can't, like I was a kid.

00:53:00.265 --> 00:53:02.228
I couldn't fully express that, you know,

00:53:02.228 --> 00:53:03.413
I can't just be like, listen,

00:53:03.413 --> 00:53:05.275
I didn't really connect with
the subject, I don't like it.

00:53:05.275 --> 00:53:06.813
- [Joey] Yeah, they can't
fend for themselves, right?

00:53:06.813 --> 00:53:08.100
- Yeah. I can't really fully explain

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:10.570
why I don't want to, why
I don't engage with this.

00:53:10.570 --> 00:53:11.403
- [Joey] Yeah.

00:53:11.403 --> 00:53:12.457
- But they'd, you know, they're just like-

00:53:12.457 --> 00:53:13.901
- But they're like, "No, we're the adults.

00:53:13.901 --> 00:53:14.734
We know more than you."

00:53:14.734 --> 00:53:15.567
- Right, right!

00:53:15.567 --> 00:53:17.108
So it's kind of a ... it's weird, man.

00:53:17.108 --> 00:53:19.331
And I think that's why like,

00:53:19.331 --> 00:53:21.891
when you get parents that
are super passionate about it

00:53:21.891 --> 00:53:24.497
and stuff, and then there's
also the thing of like,

00:53:24.497 --> 00:53:26.664
some parents don't want to

00:53:27.558 --> 00:53:29.161
get their kids diagnosed with things.

00:53:29.161 --> 00:53:30.231
'Cause they don't wanna like, be like,

00:53:30.231 --> 00:53:31.812
"My kid has a problem,"

00:53:31.812 --> 00:53:33.184
or has something, right?

00:53:33.184 --> 00:53:34.582
My parents didn't really give a shit.

00:53:34.582 --> 00:53:36.994
They were like, "If he's got
it, he's got it. Whatever."

00:53:36.994 --> 00:53:38.423
Like, just deal with it, but yeah.

00:53:38.423 --> 00:53:41.631
- Yeah, 'cause I think with
how mental health is now,

00:53:41.631 --> 00:53:43.433
it's just like, it feels like,

00:53:43.433 --> 00:53:45.019
at least it feels like,

00:53:45.019 --> 00:53:46.278
if you're diagnosed with something,

00:53:46.278 --> 00:53:47.837
then the kinda feeling is,

00:53:47.837 --> 00:53:49.552
"Oh, there's something wrong with me,"

00:53:49.552 --> 00:53:50.511
or something like that.

00:53:50.511 --> 00:53:52.724
You know, there's
something broken with me.

00:53:52.724 --> 00:53:56.368
'Cause like, with me
and ADHD, for example.

00:53:56.368 --> 00:54:01.368
Like, I didn't realize that
I was abnormal, you know?

00:54:01.432 --> 00:54:04.296
Because I would have, I
would do all of these things,

00:54:04.296 --> 00:54:05.963
to basically be able to like,

00:54:05.963 --> 00:54:08.990
seem like I was able
to function, you know?

00:54:08.990 --> 00:54:11.030
And I'd only learned recently that

00:54:11.030 --> 00:54:14.092
that was like my coping
mechanisms for like my

00:54:14.092 --> 00:54:16.574
whatever, like how my
brain works, you know?

00:54:16.574 --> 00:54:19.008
I had figured out ways to live with it.

00:54:19.008 --> 00:54:21.220
And I thought everyone had to do that.

00:54:21.220 --> 00:54:22.657
You know, that's just what I thought.

00:54:22.657 --> 00:54:24.401
And like, especially with like,

00:54:24.401 --> 00:54:25.978
after being diagnosed, it hasn't like,

00:54:25.978 --> 00:54:27.080
changed my life or anything like that.

00:54:27.080 --> 00:54:29.702
It's like, the biggest
thing that it's done for me,

00:54:29.702 --> 00:54:32.090
is like, it's just helped
me understand myself.

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:33.244
And I think that's just,

00:54:33.244 --> 00:54:34.887
I think that's like the
most valuable thing.

00:54:34.887 --> 00:54:35.720
- [Joey] Yeah.

00:54:35.720 --> 00:54:36.553
- Right?
- Definitely.

00:54:36.553 --> 00:54:37.471
- Sure. It makes sense of
things that you were like,

00:54:37.471 --> 00:54:38.488
"Why? Why am I like this?"

00:54:38.488 --> 00:54:39.820
- Yeah yeah yeah! Exactly!

00:54:39.820 --> 00:54:41.132
'Cause it's just helped me like,

00:54:41.132 --> 00:54:42.051
deal with it even more.

00:54:42.051 --> 00:54:43.805
'Cause I just remembered,

00:54:43.805 --> 00:54:46.314
because it wasn't just the
YouTube comments as well.

00:54:46.314 --> 00:54:50.326
I remember specifically
seeing this one YouTube video,

00:54:50.326 --> 00:54:52.596
and that was like the last
straw, where it was like-

00:54:52.596 --> 00:54:54.244
- Dr. Mike diagnosed me.

00:54:54.244 --> 00:54:56.189
- No no, where it was like,

00:54:56.189 --> 00:54:58.344
where it was like, "What ADHD feels like,"

00:54:58.344 --> 00:55:01.013
and someone like, edited
a video of their day,

00:55:01.013 --> 00:55:04.013
and like, edited it in a way to like

00:55:05.262 --> 00:55:06.203
show how they perceived the world.

00:55:06.203 --> 00:55:07.101
- Like thoughts and-

00:55:07.101 --> 00:55:08.568
- Yeah, how they perceived the world.

00:55:08.568 --> 00:55:12.300
And it was like, such a one for one copy

00:55:12.300 --> 00:55:15.116
of how I see the world, I was just like,

00:55:15.116 --> 00:55:16.147
"Oh my god."

00:55:16.147 --> 00:55:18.815
Like, that was like the ah-hah moment.

00:55:18.815 --> 00:55:21.670
'Cause I think there was
one scene in particular,

00:55:21.670 --> 00:55:23.263
'cause he was going about his day,

00:55:23.263 --> 00:55:26.167
and he was like, being
scolded by his teacher

00:55:26.167 --> 00:55:27.288
or his mum or something.

00:55:27.288 --> 00:55:29.794
And he was like, fidgeting, right?

00:55:29.794 --> 00:55:31.403
He was fidgeting.

00:55:31.403 --> 00:55:34.320
And he would get told off for that.

00:55:35.329 --> 00:55:37.443
Because his mum or his teacher,

00:55:37.443 --> 00:55:38.962
I can't remember who it was exactly,

00:55:38.962 --> 00:55:41.465
but they were just like,
"Can you concentrate, please?

00:55:41.465 --> 00:55:43.036
I'm trying to tell you something serious,

00:55:43.036 --> 00:55:44.291
and you're doing something else!"

00:55:44.291 --> 00:55:45.226
And I'm just like, and he said,

00:55:45.226 --> 00:55:46.614
"Oh, but it helps me to concentrate."

00:55:46.614 --> 00:55:49.471
And I'm just like, that's
been my entire life!

00:55:49.471 --> 00:55:51.168
That's been, it like,

00:55:51.168 --> 00:55:53.484
I need to fidget with something
in order to concentrate,

00:55:53.484 --> 00:55:55.297
and if I don't, I just lose like,

00:55:55.297 --> 00:55:57.183
my trail of thought or something, right?

00:55:57.183 --> 00:55:58.563
- He's just like me!

00:55:58.563 --> 00:56:01.503
- Yeah! I was just like, holy shit.

00:56:01.503 --> 00:56:04.836
Maybe I should fucking
go in and get this done.

00:56:04.836 --> 00:56:06.656
- So wait, so how do they ...

00:56:06.656 --> 00:56:08.261
'Cause I've always been curious, like,

00:56:08.261 --> 00:56:09.568
when you go see a doctor, right,

00:56:09.568 --> 00:56:10.637
and you wanna like, you know,

00:56:10.637 --> 00:56:12.563
figure out if you are
actually diagnosed with it.

00:56:12.563 --> 00:56:15.200
How does the doctor like, check that?

00:56:15.200 --> 00:56:16.033
- Well-

00:56:16.033 --> 00:56:17.016
- Is there like a test?

00:56:17.016 --> 00:56:18.903
- He just puts like a
Rubik's cube on the table

00:56:18.903 --> 00:56:20.075
and he's like ...

00:56:20.075 --> 00:56:21.196
- It's like, "I'll be back in 15 minutes."

00:56:21.196 --> 00:56:22.958
- Puts like a fidget spinner on the table.

00:56:22.958 --> 00:56:24.414
- What do you think about this?

00:56:24.414 --> 00:56:26.230
- Yeah no, 'cause I've
always been curious like,

00:56:26.230 --> 00:56:27.508
you know, how does the doctor like,

00:56:27.508 --> 00:56:30.200
kind of determine whether
you actually have ADHD

00:56:30.200 --> 00:56:31.654
or if it's just like something

00:56:31.654 --> 00:56:34.479
similar to that that's not
necessarily ADHD, right?

00:56:34.479 --> 00:56:36.877
- Yeah I mean, at the moment I'm at like,

00:56:36.877 --> 00:56:39.693
I'm still like processing
it and learning through it.

00:56:39.693 --> 00:56:42.585
'Cause at the moment, at
the beginning they take you

00:56:42.585 --> 00:56:45.153
and they do like this just,

00:56:45.153 --> 00:56:46.590
it's a survey first.

00:56:46.590 --> 00:56:48.802
So it's like the first test.

00:56:48.802 --> 00:56:52.346
Then afterwards, if you tick enough boxes

00:56:52.346 --> 00:56:54.923
to be like, "Oh okay, there's, you know-"

00:56:54.923 --> 00:56:56.250
- A chance, yeah.

00:56:56.250 --> 00:56:57.965
- There's a chance, then you
go in to the psychologist

00:56:57.965 --> 00:56:59.233
and you know, they ask you like,

00:56:59.233 --> 00:57:00.295
a bunch of questions.

00:57:00.295 --> 00:57:01.254
For me at least.

00:57:01.254 --> 00:57:02.277
I don't know, because like,

00:57:02.277 --> 00:57:04.491
another friend in England just recently

00:57:04.491 --> 00:57:06.215
got diagnosed with ADHD as well,

00:57:06.215 --> 00:57:07.192
and I think in England

00:57:07.192 --> 00:57:08.773
they have a different kind of process.

00:57:08.773 --> 00:57:09.951
- It's probably different
to every country.

00:57:09.951 --> 00:57:11.211
- Yeah yeah, I think it's
different to every country.

00:57:11.211 --> 00:57:12.997
In England I believe there's like a,

00:57:12.997 --> 00:57:15.037
you have to go to like
a group session as well,

00:57:15.037 --> 00:57:16.244
or something like that.

00:57:16.244 --> 00:57:17.092
- Oh.

00:57:17.092 --> 00:57:18.302
- It's different in every country,

00:57:18.302 --> 00:57:20.334
but for me it was more
just a one on one talk

00:57:20.334 --> 00:57:22.667
with my, you know, with the-

00:57:24.048 --> 00:57:25.003
- The psychologist.

00:57:25.003 --> 00:57:26.118
- The psychologist, where
they just asked me like,

00:57:26.118 --> 00:57:27.529
very detailed questions about

00:57:27.529 --> 00:57:30.527
my experiences, how I perceive events.

00:57:30.527 --> 00:57:32.422
And like, I can't remember everything,

00:57:32.422 --> 00:57:34.205
'cause there was like a
lot of fucking questions,

00:57:34.205 --> 00:57:35.418
but it was kinda,

00:57:35.418 --> 00:57:36.731
it was basically like a therapy session

00:57:36.731 --> 00:57:37.642
where it was just like,

00:57:37.642 --> 00:57:39.060
"So tell me about this experience."

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:40.244
- [Joey] Talk me through it.

00:57:40.244 --> 00:57:41.077
- Talk to me about this.

00:57:41.077 --> 00:57:42.402
Tell me about that.

00:57:42.402 --> 00:57:43.438
And yeah, I mean,

00:57:43.438 --> 00:57:45.921
ever since being diagnosed,

00:57:45.921 --> 00:57:47.559
I've like, like I said,

00:57:47.559 --> 00:57:48.718
my life hasn't changed that much.

00:57:48.718 --> 00:57:51.870
I just know now, it's
like written in paper,

00:57:51.870 --> 00:57:52.961
that this is-

00:57:52.961 --> 00:57:54.321
- You got the plaque now.

00:57:54.321 --> 00:57:57.089
- No no, I got the plaque of just like,

00:57:57.089 --> 00:57:59.695
"Here's how your brain works,

00:57:59.695 --> 00:58:01.907
and here's why your brain does this shit."

00:58:01.907 --> 00:58:02.740
- Yeah.

00:58:02.740 --> 00:58:04.673
- And ever since knowing that, I'm like,

00:58:04.673 --> 00:58:05.880
"Oh, that makes sense!"

00:58:05.880 --> 00:58:10.007
Now I have like a word
to put to how I felt for

00:58:10.007 --> 00:58:11.424
a lot of my life.

00:58:12.340 --> 00:58:14.004
And yeah, that's it like,

00:58:14.004 --> 00:58:14.885
that's the thing.

00:58:14.885 --> 00:58:16.040
I feel a lot of people,

00:58:16.040 --> 00:58:18.761
a lot of people maybe have, you know,

00:58:18.761 --> 00:58:20.709
maybe have different conditions

00:58:20.709 --> 00:58:22.019
or different mental conditions

00:58:22.019 --> 00:58:23.362
that they've just never
really thought about.

00:58:23.362 --> 00:58:24.846
Because, like me,

00:58:24.846 --> 00:58:27.059
maybe you found a way to
cope with it day to day,

00:58:27.059 --> 00:58:28.218
you know, because like me,

00:58:28.218 --> 00:58:29.364
maybe you thought that,

00:58:29.364 --> 00:58:30.421
"Hey, maybe everyone's like this!"

00:58:30.421 --> 00:58:32.653
And then you've just found
a way to live normally

00:58:32.653 --> 00:58:34.653
because you've grown up.

00:58:35.513 --> 00:58:37.720
You've grown up in an environment where

00:58:37.720 --> 00:58:40.025
you just need to like, just
get on with it, basically.

00:58:40.025 --> 00:58:41.446
And a lot of the times like,

00:58:41.446 --> 00:58:43.099
not every mental condition is just like,

00:58:43.099 --> 00:58:44.144
on the extreme end.

00:58:44.144 --> 00:58:45.938
Sometimes it's like, really really subtle.

00:58:45.938 --> 00:58:49.373
But if you've ever like,
had a thought that,

00:58:49.373 --> 00:58:51.580
"Maybe I do have this,"
or, "Maybe I do have that."

00:58:51.580 --> 00:58:53.810
I think it might be worth
just talking to someone.

00:58:53.810 --> 00:58:57.346
Not to like, have like a
life-changing ah-ha moment,

00:58:57.346 --> 00:58:58.679
but just learning more about yourself

00:58:58.679 --> 00:59:00.884
I feel like is the most valuable thing

00:59:00.884 --> 00:59:02.205
that someone can do.

00:59:02.205 --> 00:59:06.208
And ... doesn't mean
that there's necessarily

00:59:06.208 --> 00:59:07.606
anything wrong with you, you know?

00:59:07.606 --> 00:59:09.825
- I think more people would probably go

00:59:09.825 --> 00:59:12.628
if it was like, easier to get seen.

00:59:12.628 --> 00:59:13.461
- Yeah, definitely.

00:59:13.461 --> 00:59:15.101
- (indistinct), right, it's like.

00:59:15.101 --> 00:59:16.319
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:59:16.319 --> 00:59:17.533
- You gotta be either rich

00:59:17.533 --> 00:59:18.589
or have a lot of time on your hands.

00:59:18.589 --> 00:59:19.452
Or both.
- Or both.

00:59:19.452 --> 00:59:22.327
- To get anything done, healthcare wise,

00:59:22.327 --> 00:59:23.310
or mental health wise.

00:59:23.310 --> 00:59:25.747
I know in the UK it's not too great.

00:59:25.747 --> 00:59:28.114
I know they're like,
trying to get better at it,

00:59:28.114 --> 00:59:29.316
but I know it's not adequate.

00:59:29.316 --> 00:59:31.322
- Yeah, in the UK it's like
the waiting list is like,

00:59:31.322 --> 00:59:33.322
it's fucking, like huge.

00:59:35.633 --> 00:59:36.466
- No kidding.

00:59:36.466 --> 00:59:37.780
- Yeah, because yeah,

00:59:37.780 --> 00:59:40.219
because I feel like the biggest problem,

00:59:40.219 --> 00:59:43.316
especially like, everywhere
but America I can see,

00:59:43.316 --> 00:59:47.157
is like, access to mental
healthcare is difficult as hell.

00:59:47.157 --> 00:59:48.143
Especially in Asia.

00:59:48.143 --> 00:59:49.216
- In Japan, dude! It's like ...

00:59:49.216 --> 00:59:50.362
- Oh my god! In Japan ...

00:59:50.362 --> 00:59:51.886
- Half the time it's
just like, nonexistent.

00:59:51.886 --> 00:59:52.797
- Yeah yeah yeah.

00:59:52.797 --> 00:59:54.525
- Mental health in Japan
is the (indistinct).

00:59:54.525 --> 00:59:55.873
There is no ...

00:59:55.873 --> 00:59:56.706
(laughing)

00:59:56.706 --> 00:59:57.539
It's like, go to the pub,

00:59:57.539 --> 01:00:00.079
drink a beer, and don't think about it.

01:00:00.079 --> 01:00:02.291
- It's like, I know why you're sad!

01:00:02.291 --> 01:00:03.727
It's because you're not drunk right now.

01:00:03.727 --> 01:00:06.030
Go to an izikaya. Have a couple of beers.

01:00:06.030 --> 01:00:06.863
You'll be all right.

01:00:06.863 --> 01:00:07.771
And it's just, no.

01:00:07.771 --> 01:00:09.821
- I'm just like, I was walking home,

01:00:09.821 --> 01:00:12.410
and I saw a guy just like, on the floor.

01:00:12.410 --> 01:00:13.728
He was crying.

01:00:13.728 --> 01:00:15.598
In Tokyo, and I was like,

01:00:15.598 --> 01:00:19.110
"Why is like, no one helping him?"

01:00:19.110 --> 01:00:21.124
Like, loads of people
just walking past him,

01:00:21.124 --> 01:00:22.362
like, why is no one saying like,

01:00:22.362 --> 01:00:23.207
"Hey, are you okay?"

01:00:23.207 --> 01:00:24.237
It's just so weird to me!

01:00:24.237 --> 01:00:25.247
I was like, what?

01:00:25.247 --> 01:00:26.837
People were just like,
"Man, I don't wanna ...

01:00:26.837 --> 01:00:27.881
Don't wanna get involved."

01:00:27.881 --> 01:00:29.068
- Yeah, they don't.

01:00:29.068 --> 01:00:30.125
That's the thing.

01:00:30.125 --> 01:00:30.958
- [Connor] That's so fucking sad.

01:00:30.958 --> 01:00:31.991
- They see, 'cause you know, again.

01:00:31.991 --> 01:00:34.681
Collectivist society,
they see one, you know,

01:00:34.681 --> 01:00:37.114
cog that's out of the
system, and they're like,

01:00:37.114 --> 01:00:38.381
"Well, that's not our problem.

01:00:38.381 --> 01:00:40.288
We just gotta keep the system moving."

01:00:40.288 --> 01:00:42.000
- Well the UK isn't great, but I think,

01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:43.076
at least where I was from,

01:00:43.076 --> 01:00:44.262
if someone was like, crying in the street,

01:00:44.262 --> 01:00:45.412
like everyone would be
like, "Are you okay?"

01:00:45.412 --> 01:00:46.591
- Oh yeah!
- Yeah yeah yeah!

01:00:46.591 --> 01:00:47.779
- I'd say most English-speaking countries

01:00:47.779 --> 01:00:49.777
there would definitely be
at least a couple people

01:00:49.777 --> 01:00:52.514
being like, "Hey, you all
right dude? What's up?"

01:00:52.514 --> 01:00:53.956
- At least like, someone checking on them.

01:00:53.956 --> 01:00:55.135
- Yeah! Exactly.

01:00:55.135 --> 01:00:57.088
And you know, not to say that Japan isn't,

01:00:57.088 --> 01:00:59.359
you know, the fucking
cruel mistress of the world

01:00:59.359 --> 01:01:00.260
in that sense, right?

01:01:00.260 --> 01:01:01.514
Like there's definitely people,

01:01:01.514 --> 01:01:02.433
I've seen people, you know,

01:01:02.433 --> 01:01:03.593
go up to strangers and be like, you know.

01:01:03.593 --> 01:01:04.426
(speaks foreign language)

01:01:04.426 --> 01:01:05.440
- I wanted to help, but I was like,

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:09.262
I don't want this to be a awkward
language barrier situation

01:01:09.262 --> 01:01:10.888
where you actually need help

01:01:10.888 --> 01:01:12.204
but I'm just like,
(speaks foreign language).

01:01:12.204 --> 01:01:13.037
You know?

01:01:13.037 --> 01:01:14.033
I felt so sad about that,

01:01:14.033 --> 01:01:15.631
I couldn't do anything to help this dude.

01:01:15.631 --> 01:01:17.314
'Cause I was like, I don't know,

01:01:17.314 --> 01:01:19.676
just imagining what situations
he could be going through

01:01:19.676 --> 01:01:20.596
made him so fucking sad.

01:01:20.596 --> 01:01:22.347
I was like, what if his parents like,

01:01:22.347 --> 01:01:24.600
said he couldn't go home or something.

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:25.739
I don't fucking know, I was like,

01:01:25.739 --> 01:01:26.750
ugh, fuck it!

01:01:26.750 --> 01:01:27.796
- [Joey] Yeah man.

01:01:27.796 --> 01:01:30.128
- Yeah. I just wanted to add
an addendum to what I said.

01:01:30.128 --> 01:01:32.024
If you do, like,

01:01:32.024 --> 01:01:34.246
I think a big issue
nowadays as well is like,

01:01:34.246 --> 01:01:36.527
there's a lot of self-diagnoses.

01:01:36.527 --> 01:01:39.301
Like, especially, I've seen
it on TikTok especially.

01:01:39.301 --> 01:01:40.349
- The internet doctors.

01:01:40.349 --> 01:01:42.561
- Like, they're like, I've
read one Google article.

01:01:42.561 --> 01:01:43.998
I am, like, you know,

01:01:43.998 --> 01:01:46.045
I'm like, I got this ADHD,

01:01:46.045 --> 01:01:47.188
or I'm like ...

01:01:47.188 --> 01:01:49.445
- Well it's almost like
the, what's the word?

01:01:49.445 --> 01:01:51.658
The like collection of, you know,

01:01:51.658 --> 01:01:53.251
people kind of, say what you will,

01:01:53.251 --> 01:01:55.462
but online they kinda see
it as like a badge of honor.

01:01:55.462 --> 01:01:56.295
Sometimes.

01:01:56.295 --> 01:01:57.195
- Oh yeah yeah yeah!
